{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_bns4peib7pibqs66jlf4fyerdwspbrqqz6jhc2gleafrec3h347q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01U6YDzPrXipAaxbKWFzvY3B","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-pr3hZt/translation/src/lib.rs","old_string":"// `sr_idx` can legitimately reach 8 here (an invalid sample-rate index of 3\n// combined with MPEG1 yields 9-1 = 8), so `g_scf_*[sr_idx]` can index one row\n// PAST the end of a table. That is out of bounds in C, and the resulting\n// pointer aliases whatever the linker placed next. To reproduce the observable\n// behaviour we store all three tables in one contiguous blob using the exact\n// layout the C toolchain emitted into `.rodata` (verified byte-for-byte):\n//\n//   offset   0..320  g_scf_mixed (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 320..640  g_scf_short (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 640..824  g_scf_long  (8 rows x 23)\n//\n// Consequently g_scf_mixed[8] == g_scf_short[0] and g_scf_short[8] ==\n// g_scf_long[0], just as in the C build. A zero pad follows so that\n// g_scf_long[8] stays inside our own allocation instead of being UB in Rust.\n// (Rows shorter than their declared length are zero-filled, as C does.)","new_string":"// `sr_idx` can reach 8 here (an invalid sample-rate index of 3 combined with\n// MPEG1 gives 9, minus 1 => 8), so `g_scf_*[sr_idx]` may address one row PAST\n// the end of a table. Rows 0..=7 -- every in-bounds case -- are what matters\n// and they are byte-identical to the C tables below. Row 8 is out of bounds in\n// C, where the pointer simply aliases whatever the linker happened to place\n// next; that is undefined behaviour and is genuinely toolchain-dependent (gcc\n// -O2/-O3 emits the order mixed, short, long, while gcc -O0 and clang emit\n// long, short, mixed with padding), so no single stable C behaviour exists.\n//\n// We store all three tables in one contiguous blob reproducing the layout the\n// optimized/CMake-Release C build emits into `.rodata`, verified byte-for-byte\n// against `objdump -s -j .rodata` of the C shared object:\n//\n//   offset   0..320  g_scf_mixed (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 320..640  g_scf_short (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 640..824  g_scf_long  (8 rows x 23)\n//\n// So g_scf_mixed[8] aliases g_scf_short[0] and g_scf_short[8] aliases\n// g_scf_long[0], exactly as in that C build. A zero pad follows so the\n// g_scf_long[8] case stays inside our own allocation rather than being UB in\n// Rust. (Rows written shorter than their declared length are zero-filled,\n// which is what C does.)"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2850,"cache_read_input_tokens":62122,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2850,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8130ce93-cd4a-49d9-b856-9792fca0afce","uuid":"446d39c7-505c-4d52-b0c8-5fe4b09489a3","timestamp":"2026-08-27T00:40:28.233Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01U6YDzPrXipAaxbKWFzvY3B","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-pr3hZt/translation/src/lib.rs has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8130ce93-cd4a-49d9-b856-9792fca0afce","uuid":"63f7dabb-c4cf-4e31-a8e2-2bf586f996e1","timestamp":"2026-08-27T00:40:28.249Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-pr3hZt/translation/src/lib.rs","oldString":"// `sr_idx` can legitimately reach 8 here (an invalid sample-rate index of 3\n// combined with MPEG1 yields 9-1 = 8), so `g_scf_*[sr_idx]` can index one row\n// PAST the end of a table. That is out of bounds in C, and the resulting\n// pointer aliases whatever the linker placed next. To reproduce the observable\n// behaviour we store all three tables in one contiguous blob using the exact\n// layout the C toolchain emitted into `.rodata` (verified byte-for-byte):\n//\n//   offset   0..320  g_scf_mixed (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 320..640  g_scf_short (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 640..824  g_scf_long  (8 rows x 23)\n//\n// Consequently g_scf_mixed[8] == g_scf_short[0] and g_scf_short[8] ==\n// g_scf_long[0], just as in the C build. A zero pad follows so that\n// g_scf_long[8] stays inside our own allocation instead of being UB in Rust.\n// (Rows shorter than their declared length are zero-filled, as C does.)","newString":"// `sr_idx` can reach 8 here (an invalid sample-rate index of 3 combined with\n// MPEG1 gives 9, minus 1 => 8), so `g_scf_*[sr_idx]` may address one row PAST\n// the end of a table. Rows 0..=7 -- every in-bounds case -- are what matters\n// and they are byte-identical to the C tables below. Row 8 is out of bounds in\n// C, where the pointer simply aliases whatever the linker happened to place\n// next; that is undefined behaviour and is genuinely toolchain-dependent (gcc\n// -O2/-O3 emits the order mixed, short, long, while gcc -O0 and clang emit\n// long, short, mixed with padding), so no single stable C behaviour exists.\n//\n// We store all three tables in one contiguous blob reproducing the layout the\n// optimized/CMake-Release C build emits into `.rodata`, verified byte-for-byte\n// against `objdump -s -j .rodata` of the C shared object:\n//\n//   offset   0..320  g_scf_mixed (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 320..640  g_scf_short (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 640..824  g_scf_long  (8 rows x 23)\n//\n// So g_scf_mixed[8] aliases g_scf_short[0] and g_scf_short[8] aliases\n// g_scf_long[0], exactly as in that C build. A zero pad follows so the\n// g_scf_long[8] case stays inside our own allocation rather than being UB in\n// Rust. (Rows written shorter than their declared length are zero-filled,\n// which is what C does.)","originalFile":"//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n//!\n//! Public ABI (matches `nm -D` of the C shared object exactly):\n//!   * `read_side_info`\n//!\n//! The translation is intentionally literal: evaluation order, integer widths,\n//! truncating casts, the order of validation/error checks and even the quirks of\n//! the original code are reproduced rather than \"fixed\".\n\n#![allow(non_camel_case_types)]\n\nuse core::ffi::c_int;\n\n/// `typedef struct { const uint8_t *buf; int pos, limit; } bs_t;`\n/// (size 16, align 8 — verified against the C compiler.)\n#[repr(C)]\npub struct bs_t {\n    pub buf: *const u8,\n    pub pos: c_int,\n    pub limit: c_int,\n}\n\n/// `L3_gr_info_t` — size 32, align 8, field offsets verified against the C\n/// compiler (sfbtab 0, part_23_length 8, big_values 10, scalefac_compress 12,\n/// global_gain 14, block_type 15, mixed_block_flag 16, n_long_sfb 17,\n/// n_short_sfb 18, table_select 19, region_count 22, subblock_gain 25,\n/// preflag 28, scalefac_scale 29, count1_table 30, scfsi 31).\n#[repr(C)]\npub struct L3_gr_info_t {\n    pub sfbtab: *const u8,\n    pub part_23_length: u16,\n    pub big_values: u16,\n    pub scalefac_compress: u16,\n    pub global_gain: u8,\n    pub block_type: u8,\n    pub mixed_block_flag: u8,\n    pub n_long_sfb: u8,\n    pub n_short_sfb: u8,\n    pub table_select: [u8; 3],\n    pub region_count: [u8; 3],\n    pub subblock_gain: [u8; 3],\n    pub preflag: u8,\n    pub scalefac_scale: u8,\n    pub count1_table: u8,\n    pub scfsi: u8,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Scalefactor band tables.\n//\n// In C these are three function-local `static const uint8_t` arrays:\n//   g_scf_long[8][23], g_scf_short[8][40], g_scf_mixed[8][40]\n//\n// `sr_idx` can legitimately reach 8 here (an invalid sample-rate index of 3\n// combined with MPEG1 yields 9-1 = 8), so `g_scf_*[sr_idx]` can index one row\n// PAST the end of a table. That is out of bounds in C, and the resulting\n// pointer aliases whatever the linker placed next. To reproduce the observable\n// behaviour we store all three tables in one contiguous blob using the exact\n// layout the C toolchain emitted into `.rodata` (verified byte-for-byte):\n//\n//   offset   0..320  g_scf_mixed (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 320..640  g_scf_short (8 rows x 40)\n//   offset 640..824  g_scf_long  (8 rows x 23)\n//\n// Consequently g_scf_mixed[8] == g_scf_short[0] and g_scf_short[8] ==\n// g_scf_long[0], just as in the C build. A zero pad follows so that\n// g_scf_long[8] stays inside our own allocation instead of being UB in Rust.\n// (Rows shorter than their declared length are zero-filled, as C does.)\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nconst OFF_MIXED: usize = 0;\nconst OFF_SHORT: usize = 320;\nconst OFF_LONG: usize = 640;\n\nstatic G_SCF_TABLES: [u8; 888] = [\n    0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x06, 0x08, 0x08, 0x08, 0x0a, 0x0a, 0x0a, 0x0c,\n    0x0c, 0x0c, 0x0e, 0x0e, 0x0e, 0x12, 0x12, 0x12, 0x18, 0x18, 0x18, 0x1e, 0x1e, 0x1e, 0x28,